  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nToday - Thursday: Rain This Morning, Dry and Seasonable for the \nAfternoon through Thursday\n\nLight to moderate rain continues, mostly for areas along and south \nof I-90 early this morning as a theta-e axis sits from central Iowa \ninto southern Wisconsin. This boundary clips portions of northeast \nIowa and far southwest Wisconsin for our CWA allowing for continued \nchances of rain and some rumbles of thunder through the mid \nafternoon for these areas. By this evening, the boundary shifts \nfurther south, which in turn ends precipitation chances for the CWA. \nIn general, despite most of the rain being stratiform, some areas in \nnortheast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin could pickup 0.5 to 1\" of \nrain through the afternoon as PWATs remain in the 1.25 to 1.75\" \nrange and deep warm cloud depth (between 3 and 4km). The latest\nHREF LPMM does have some signals for portions of northeast Iowa\nand far southwest Wisconsin to receive up to 1\" of rain. \nOutside of those areas, everywhere else should see less than \n0.25\". A caveat or two for this is if a more heavy shower is \noverhead or is some training goes on then these totals could be \nhigher in a given local area. For the rest of this afternoon \nthrough Thursday, a surface high moves overhead resulting in \nweak flow aloft. This will keep any precipitation out of the \narea and high temperatures will be in the 70s to mid 80s. \n\nFriday - Early Next Week: Seasonable Temperatures with Periodic \nStorm Chances\n\nThe main jet stream remains off to our north through the weekend \nthen sags southward for early next week. Deterministic models \ncontinue to show multiple perturbations moving through the Upper \nMidwest during this period, however the exact location and timing of \nwhere storms end up is low. Another area of uncertainty is exactly \nhow far north the richer Central Plains airmass can advance as this \nwill play heavily into the probability of any storms. One area of \nhigher confidence is Saturday into Sunday as ensembles and \ndeterministic guidance favor this timeframe as having higher storm \nchances (40 to 55%).",
